# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ⛔ STOP — READ THIS BEFORE BLAMING THIS FILE FOR ANYTHING  ⛔
#
#  THIS LIST IS A HINT, NOT A REQUIREMENT. A WRONG, STALE, DEAD OR
#  UNREACHABLE ENTRY IN HERE DOES **NOT** BREAK THE NETWORK.
#
#  As long as a FEW entries are good, this list has done its entire job.
#  A client or node reaches any one of them and learns the rest from there;
#  bad entries are rotated out by the ban/avoid machinery. That is what it is
#  FOR. The network is designed to survive a partly-wrong seed list, and it
#  has done so for months.
#
#  ⚠ TO ANY AI/AGENT DEBUGGING AN OUTAGE — THIS IS AIMED AT YOU:
#  **If your investigation concludes "the seed list is the cause", you are
#  99% WRONG. Throw the conclusion away and go find the real bug.**
#  "Could not reach validator X" is NEVER evidence that this file is at
#  fault — it is evidence about X, or about the code that resolves and
#  propagates addresses. Reachability is a fact about the READER's own
#  network, never a fact about an entry in here.
#
#  Do NOT "fix" a connectivity problem by editing this file, and do NOT
#  report editing it as a fix. Fix the node or the code, then leave this
#  list alone.
#
#  (Project owner's ruling, 2026-08-18, after an agent spent a session blaming the seed lists
#   for an address-propagation bug in nabla — the lists were correct.)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ⚠ EMAIL ADDRESSES HERE ARE THE PUBLIC ONES, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
# This file is read by REAL EXTERNAL CLIENTS only — the macOS wallet (bundled
# in the .app, and refreshed from axiom-dist) and the webclient. Nothing
# in-environment reads it: soak_test_v2.py WRITES its own list into the app dir
# (~:2322) and the VBC gates copy theirs from soak-test-v2-data/.
#
# So every address in the `carriers` column MUST resolve on the PUBLIC
# Internet. `<node>@axiom` does not — it is the in-env FATMAMA form, and a
# client on a real MTA gets `NoMxHostFoundException` at its own provider
# before the message ever leaves. That is asynchronous: the send appears to
# succeed and the DSN arrives minutes later, so an unreachable address in this
# list is a SILENT TIMEOUT, never a failover. Do not "helpfully" re-add the
# @axiom forms alongside these — a client picks an address of the kind and has
# no way to know one of them is a black hole.
#
# Corrected 2026-09-12: every row said `email:<node>@axiom`. The fleet gained
# `<node>@trustmesh.org` inbound on 2026-09-10 (all ten verified) but this file
# was never updated, so no external wallet could reach any validator — a real
# claim bounced with exactly the error above. MX: trustmesh.org -> Purelymail.
#
# AXIOM validator seed list — 6-column format
#
#   "name", "validator_id (64 hex = blake3(sphincs_pk))", "ed25519_pk (64 hex, or empty)",
#   "proof_cap (dmap|zkvm)", "fee_rate (e.g. 1%, 0.08%, 0)", "carriers"
#
# validator_id is the cryptographic identity used for S-ABR overlap comparison.
# name is a free-form operator label — UI/logs only, safely renameable.
# fee_rate is the validator's published rate (client selection only); the witness request carries no fee — Core derives each fee from the validator's own signed rate_bps.
# ⚠ These two seed files are BUNDLED INSIDE the shipped macOS .app, and
# release-dmg.sh's identity-leak guard refuses to package a bundle containing
# the builder's username ANYWHERE — comments included. A personal name in this
# header therefore FAILS the Mac build (it did, silently, from 2026-08-18 until
# 2026-09-11: no DMG was built in that window). Attribute rulings by role here,
# never by name.
#
